    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d289">

                <pb facs="0350" n="1184" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1184"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 657.119/487b</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Great
                        Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-05-06T19:00:00-04:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 6, 1918, 7 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>7611. For Sheldon [from War Trade Board]:</p>
                <p>No. 434. Embassy’s 9700, April 24, 6 p.m., your 477.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d289-f1"/> Only 50,000 tons Norwegian sailing vessels available so
                    far. Balance will be forthcoming gradually at later date up to 200,000 tons. All
                    chartered for safe trades, principally east and west coast South America and
                    Australia. Steamer tonnage which they will take place of has already been
                    released from these trades.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
